<p>New video, satellite imagery, and photos circulated by Iranian media are being cited by multiple outlets as evidence that a U.S.-made Tomahawk cruise missile struck a military compound in Minab, southern Iran, near an elementary school hit in the Feb. 28 blast that Iranian authorities say killed roughly 165–175 people, many of them children. Analysts cited in these reports say the likely target was nearby military infrastructure, including a communications tower and an Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps-linked naval base, with the school next to the compound. The U.S. Defense Department says it is investigating. President Trump has suggested Iran may have carried out the strike—or that it may have been done by “somebody else”—and has also claimed Iran could have used a Tomahawk-type weapon. Separate reporting says U.S. investigators have made a preliminary assessment that American forces may have been responsible, though no final conclusion has been announced publicly. U.S. Democrats and human rights advocates have called for an impartial inquiry, reflecting a shared push—across very different political viewpoints—for clearer accountability and stronger safeguards for civilians as the war widens.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/DnVJaLx0Qxc95sk-KYiMIe1GB89ABblaH7dEwWVKaFgV0VfxinG7ff007uhgPvaYku3_FYCtiip-WIJokHBw1IC88WFFOjj6n8JwnNFhRa9HiiW8gi46bwKjGr1UNtZ9zJGMjptD_eYF8_sF1M8jdv3E_GU_FE-6XUFc2uUtt0L3-C45PFPQQrF2kbLqt66gLRbUNIACXPv9e84VkvX74-X-WO1URhGGPsF7M930rG5bwBoPRtX4CAdeXtbgqbff47zZ8E-Lsw4QStE' alt='Mourners at a funeral in Iran after the Minab school strike, according to Reuters imagery used by The Hindu.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Verification chain: Multiple newsrooms treated the clip released by Iran’s semiofficial Mehr News Agency as authentic. Its geolocation and timing then became a key input for open-source reviews of what happened at the Minab site.</li><li>Likely aimpoint: Analysts reviewing satellite imagery pointed to a communications tower near the compound as a possible target, which could explain why the impact point appears offset from the school buildings.</li><li>Limits on independent confirmation: Outside journalists and monitors have faced access constraints, making key details—including casualty claims and the precise circumstances—hard to verify independently.</li><li>Political response details: A group of Democratic lawmakers said they were “horrified” and called for an impartial investigation, framing their demand around accountability and civilian-protection safeguards as the war expands.</li><li>Wider war effects: As fighting with Iran widened, The Guardian reported that oil prices surged, raising the economic stakes alongside the military and diplomatic fallout from the incident.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Iranian authorities: Iran has accused Israel and the United States of striking an elementary school in Minab and has reported a death toll above 150. Iranian media released video and photos of debris to support its account. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/03/what-we-know-about-alleged-strike-iran-school'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li><li>Pentagon leadership: U.S. officials say the Defense Department is investigating what happened. (<a href='https://theweek.com/world-news/iran-minab-school-strike'>The Week</a>)</li><li>President Donald Trump: President Trump said he believed Iran carried out the strike, and separately suggested Iran or “somebody else” could have done it. He also claimed Iran had access to Tomahawk missiles. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/mar/09/trump-iran-war-oil'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>U.S. Democratic lawmakers: Democratic lawmakers said they were “horrified” by the bombing and urged an impartial investigation into the incident. (<a href='https://www.firstpost.com/world/democrats-demand-probe-into-attack-on-iran-school-as-trump-says-he-doesnt-know-enough-about-it-13987869.html'>Firstpost</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3